#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/solvers/instability-nonsmooth-functions.ipynb:70
msgid "Smooth approximations to non-differentiable functions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/solvers/instability-nonsmooth-functions.ipynb:81
msgid ""
"Some functions, such as ``minimum``, ``maximum``, ``heaviside``, and "
"``abs``, are discontinuous and/or non-differentiable (their derivative is "
"discontinuous). Adaptive solvers can deal with this discontinuity, but will "
"take many more steps close to the discontinuity in order to resolve it. "
"Therefore, using soft approximations instead can reduce the number of steps "
"taken by the solver, and hence the integration time. See `this post <https://"
"discourse.julialang.org/t/handling-instability-when-solving-ode-"
"problems/9019/5>`__ for more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/solvers/instability-nonsmooth-functions.ipynb:84
msgid ""
"Here is an example using the ``maximum`` function. The function "
"``maximum(x,1)`` is continuous but non-differentiable at ``x=1``, where its "
"derivative jumps from 0 to 1. However, we can approximate it using the "
"```softplus`` function <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/"
"Rectifier_(neural_networks)#Softplus>`__, which is smooth everywhere and is "
"sometimes used in neural networks as a smooth approximation to the RELU "
"activation function. The ``softplus`` function is given by"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/solvers/instability-nonsmooth-functions.ipynb:86
msgid "s(x,y;k) = \\frac{\\log(\\exp(kx)+\\exp(ky))}{k},"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/solvers/instability-nonsmooth-functions.ipynb:91
msgid ""
"where ``k`` is a strictly positive smoothing (or sharpness) parameter. The "
"larger the value of ``k``, the better the approximation but the stiffer the "
"term (exp blows up quickly!). Usually, a value of ``k=10`` is a good middle "
"ground."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/solvers/instability-nonsmooth-functions.ipynb:93
msgid ""
"In PyBaMM, you can either call the ``softplus`` function directly, or change "
"``pybamm.settings.max_smoothing`` to automatically replace all your calls to "
"``pybamm.maximum`` with ``softplus``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/solvers/instability-nonsmooth-functions.ipynb:-1
msgid "[2]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/solvers/instability-nonsmooth-functions.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"x = pybamm.Variable(\"x\")\n"
"y = pybamm.Variable(\"y\")\n"
"\n"
"# Normal maximum\n"
"print(f\"Exact maximum: {pybamm.maximum(x, y)}\")\n"
"\n"
"# Softplus\n"
"print(\"Softplus (k=10): \", pybamm.softplus(x, y, 10))\n"
"\n"
"# Changing the setting to call softplus automatically\n"
"pybamm.settings.min_max_mode = \"soft\"\n"
"pybamm.settings.min_max_smoothing = 20\n"
"print(f\"Softplus (k=20): {pybamm.maximum(x, y)}\")\n"
"\n"
"# All smoothing parameters can be changed at once\n"
"pybamm.settings.set_smoothing_parameters(30)\n"
"print(f\"Softplus (k=30): {pybamm.maximum(x, y)}\")\n"
"\n"
"# Change back\n"
"pybamm.settings.set_smoothing_parameters(\"exact\")\n"
"print(f\"Exact maximum: {pybamm.maximum(x, y)}\")"
msgstr ""

